WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:05.171
Standby for adventure!

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:13.847
With conventional fuel rockets,

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:17.384
it would take 15, 16,000 years
to get to the nearest star.

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:19.953
Conventional kind of rockets

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:22.389
are just out of the question
for interstellar travel.

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:23.456
You're gonna have to do something

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:24.858
a little bit more out there.

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:28.461
Slow to half impulse.

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:29.963
Going one half impulse.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.971
What we're trying to do
is build an impulse engine

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.472
like you hear on "Star Trek."

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.074
We'd like it to go at about a 0.4

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:41.975
the velocity of light,

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:43.309
just so that we can get
to the nearest stars

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.511
within a human lifetime.

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:48.014
There's no burnt up fuel.

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.316
There's nothing being
shot out of a tailpipe.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.584
And it's the gravitational field

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:53.820
that actually will propel
this thing forward.

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:58.358
This is the Mach Effect
Gravity Assist Drive,

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:02.529
a device that could make
interstellar space travel possible.

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:04.497
But the MEGA drive, as it's known,

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:05.598
is also a design

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:09.269
that hinges on some pretty
controversial physics.

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:10.870
For many years,

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.940
you work on a project
that people think is nuts

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.743
and you're likely to be
regarded as nuts too.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.080
Now, after 30 years of fine-tuning,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.415
this pair of scientists
might finally be close

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.951
to getting the results
they've been hoping for.

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:27.921
And NASA are taking the idea seriously.

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:31.791
If something like that were
true, it'd be earth-shaking.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:35.428
It'd be groundbreaking,
revolutionary new physics,

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:40.066
but those sorts of advancements
don't come along very often.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:41.768
That would be a pretty extraordinary claim

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:44.270
to say that we've put this new spin

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.472
on the way the universe works

00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:48.675
that we didn't know before.

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:58.184
It's a well-known Newtonian law:

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.487
an object at rest stays at rest,

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:04.390
and an object in motion stays
in motion at a constant speed

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.393
unless an external force acts on it.

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:10.363
All objects resist a push to some extent,

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:11.898
and what makes them do that?

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:12.732
Inertia.

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:16.469
In the 19th century

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:19.105
Austrian physicist Ernst
Mach made a conjecture

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:21.007
that these forces of inertia

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:24.911
result from the gravity of
objects in the distant universe.

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:27.480
It became known as Mach's principle.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.183
And while most experts
have now dismissed it,

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:32.152
scientists at this lab in California

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:34.454
think the idea is just misunderstood.

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.192
Mach's principle itself has
sort of gone out of vogue

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.261
and people don't seriously even discuss

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:43.463
Mach's principal anymore.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:45.765
It just depends on the
way you think about it.

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:46.933
If you're looking at a textbook,

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:48.902
you might find five or
six different definitions

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:49.903
of Mach's principle.

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:51.137
But the one I'm thinking of

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:54.340
is that distant matter way out there

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.276
can influence things up close.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.511
To get your head around it,

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:00.680
take this old analogy used to illustrate

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.949
how matter bends space-time.

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:06.719
If you put a heavy object on a trampoline,

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.255
it falls in and curves the rubber sheet.

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:11.991
Now, if you roll a ball on the trampoline,

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.961
it will keep orbiting the
heavy mass in the center.

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:16.496
That's how a planet behaves

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:19.465
when it's attracted by
the gravity of a star.

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:21.868
The thing is for the rubber
sheet to act that way,

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.169
it has to be under tension.

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:24.470
It has to be stretched.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.572
And we're saying that basically,

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:27.273
the distant matter of the universe

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:30.710
is what's pulling the
space-time and making it taut.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.412
And that's what's
causing it to act like a,

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:33.880
basically like a rubber sheet.

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:36.149
The stretched rubber sheet

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:38.585
is loaded with potential energy.

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:40.186
And according to the team's understanding

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:41.621
of the Mach principle,

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:43.523
so is space-time.

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:44.424
So we're thinking

00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:46.893
that there's a big gravitational
potential out there

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:48.728
and this device can
actually tap into that.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:52.565
Imagine the space-time of the universe

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.734
as being like a very, very flat lake.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.703
And you're sitting in a rowboat,

00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:57.737
the kind that has the seat

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.372
that moves backwards and forwards,

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:00.707
but you don't have any oars.

00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:02.375
Instead, you have two great big buckets,

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:04.477
like big trash cans.

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:06.246
While you slide forward in the seat,

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:09.449
the mass shifts around and
the boat moves slightly.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:13.186
That's conservation of
momentum, another Newtonian law.

00:04:13.186 --> 00:04:15.922
If there are no external
forces acting on a system

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:16.923
while it's moving,

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:19.892
its center of mass has
to stay in the same spot.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.194
And so the boat is moving

00:04:21.194 --> 00:04:24.530
to compensate for the
different distribution of mass.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:25.465
Now, what you're gonna do is

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:27.367
you're gonna dump the
buckets into the water,

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.801
which is the universal space-time.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.271
And now, you're a lot
heavier than you were.

00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:32.872
Now, you're gonna slide back in the seat.

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:34.474
And as you slide back,

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:36.175
the boat is gonna move under you

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:38.811
so that your center of mass
remains in the same spot.

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:40.179
The boat actually moves forward

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.215
as you slide back in the seat.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:43.516
Then you can dump out the water,

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:44.450
'cause you don't need it anymore,

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.152
and then you repeat that process.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:52.258
The MEGA drive works in a similar way.

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:53.960
The part which is doing all the work

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.963
is a stack of piezoelectric crystals,

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.398
the same kind of material

00:04:58.398 --> 00:05:00.199
that's in your electric toothbrush.

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:04.003
When you apply a certain
frequency of electric current,

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:06.272
the crystals expand and contract,

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:09.108
which causes the whole device to vibrate.

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:10.510
But the MEGA drive is set up

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.845
to vibrate mainly in one direction,

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:15.615
which causes it to accelerate slightly.

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:18.685
The team says this
acceleration enables the device

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:21.888
to tap into the gravitational
potential of the universe.

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:24.090
By borrowing some of its energy,

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.425
the crystals change mass

00:05:25.425 --> 00:05:28.528
and the device starts
behaving like our rowboat.

00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:30.663
That mass change is what's
causing the propulsion

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.433
because each crystal kind of
moves in this sort of fashion.

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:35.335
So the whole stack is
in this breathing mode,

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:37.103
which moves backwards and forwards.

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:40.540
As it gains energy, it increases mass.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.710
And then it shrinks again and loses mass.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.845
If you time it just right,

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:48.147
you can actually make
that thing move forward.

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:54.420
All of this

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.322
has been the life's work of Jim Woodward,

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:59.492
who set off on this path
over three decades ago.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.662
Since then, he's been working alone

00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:05.365
on the MEGA drive
project in his spare time

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:08.201
until his desk at the
university was relocated

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:10.536
to the office of Hal Fearn.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:11.704
I was away on sabbatical.

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:14.006
And I came back and there he was.

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:16.109
There I was.

00:06:16.109 --> 00:06:18.711
And Hal was not happy.

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:20.546
And at first, I thought
it was absolutely insane.

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.782
I thought, what on earth
is this guy trying to do?

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:24.751
I took a closer look at what he was doing.

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:26.819
That's when I really
became more interested.

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:28.855
The shift is very small though.

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:30.523
The shift is not that small.

00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:32.125
I grew up on "Star Trek"

00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:34.127
and I would love to be able to say

00:06:34.127 --> 00:06:35.061
that I worked on something

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:37.964
that would help people to get
across space-time quickly.

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:39.732
If the theory turns out to be correct

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:42.368
and we are really tapping
into the gravitational field,

00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:44.404
it would be a serious breakthrough.

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:46.005
It's just, I've got to prove it.

00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:50.410
Hi, Shell, are you there?
Yeah, I'm here.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:53.079
But proving this idea has been a problem.

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:57.483
The movements of the device
have been extremely small,

00:06:57.483 --> 00:07:00.620
not large enough to
register with the naked eye.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.321
But after receiving two grants

00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:04.991
from the NASA Institute
for Advanced Concepts,

00:07:04.991 --> 00:07:06.526
or NIAC for short,

00:07:06.526 --> 00:07:08.428
the team has managed to greatly improve

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:10.496
their existing prototype.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.765
Jim was basically paying all
this out of his own money.

00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:16.035
He must have put 30 to
$50,000 into this project.

00:07:16.035 --> 00:07:18.538
He didn't want to buy off
the shelf kind of equipment

00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:20.139
'cause some of it wasn't available

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.308
when he started doing
this 30 odd years ago.

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:23.743
You couldn't buy some of the equipment.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:26.913
You just had to make it and
build the circuits yourself.

00:07:26.913 --> 00:07:29.515
We did have some people
come over at one point

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:32.652
and they saw Jim's homemade boxes.

00:07:32.652 --> 00:07:34.587
They didn't really take
him that seriously.

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:38.257
I had to convince Jim to buy
some equipment off the shelf

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:40.259
that we could get fully calibrated.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:42.795
Trying to get things set up

00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:46.799
so that we could actually do some runs

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:51.304
that would make
interesting video material.

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:52.705
Many new collaborators

00:07:52.705 --> 00:07:54.006
have joined along the way,

00:07:54.006 --> 00:07:57.143
and the team has designed
a new setup for the device,

00:07:57.143 --> 00:07:59.979
hanging it from a pendulum
to increase thrust

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:01.747
and eliminate any vibrations,

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:03.950
which might be mistaken for it.

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:05.451
Okay, here it goes.

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:09.055
Three, two, one.

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:13.926
And they finally started seeing it move

00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:15.428
with their own eyes.

00:08:15.428 --> 00:08:16.696
You can physically see this thing

00:08:16.696 --> 00:08:18.231
shudder around, vibrating,

00:08:18.231 --> 00:08:20.399
and you can physically see
it move off to one side.

00:08:20.399 --> 00:08:21.968
So that's actually quite exciting.

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:24.403
If we can actually show that by video,

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:25.771
then I think that actually should convince

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:27.039
quite a few people.

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:29.775
It’s on a reflector on the frame.

00:08:29.775 --> 00:08:30.743
Okay?

00:08:30.743 --> 00:08:33.079
I still think we should
give Jim that air horn

00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:34.480
that looks like a wormhole.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.581
No.

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:36.782
'Cause I think it looks cool.

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:37.617
Yeah.

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:43.322
The devices we build now
produce those type of forces

00:08:43.322 --> 00:08:47.059
between 100 and 1,000 times larger

00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:49.362
than the forces that we were producing

00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:51.597
even a year or two ago.

00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:52.965
It's very, very small.

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:54.767
If you think maybe a little pulley

00:08:54.767 --> 00:08:57.703
with a little mass held
on the end of a string,

00:08:57.703 --> 00:08:58.905
the force that that would produce

00:08:58.905 --> 00:09:01.941
if you dropped, say, two or
three grams of weight down.

00:09:01.941 --> 00:09:04.043
You'd almost barely feel it in your hand.

00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:05.711
It's almost like the weight of a feather.

00:09:05.711 --> 00:09:06.946
Maybe a little bit more than a feather.

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:08.614
Maybe two feathers.

00:09:08.614 --> 00:09:09.448
I'll be be generous,

00:09:09.448 --> 00:09:11.150
I'll give you a couple of feathers.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.785
The team calculates

00:09:12.785 --> 00:09:14.186
that the smaller the device,

00:09:14.186 --> 00:09:16.255
the larger the force it can generate.

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:17.924
So instead of scaling up,

00:09:17.924 --> 00:09:20.960
they hope that arrays of
thousands of tiny MEGA drives

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.063
powered by a nuclear battery
could one day be deployed

00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:27.233
to accelerate large probes
into interstellar space.

00:09:28.301 --> 00:09:30.703
They are sufficient actually
in and of themselves

00:09:30.703 --> 00:09:35.174
to make it possible to take a starship,

00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:39.045
a human-crewed starship, to nearby stars

00:09:39.045 --> 00:09:42.648
and back in some reasonable
fraction of the human lifetime.

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:45.718
So the question is,
have we all screwed up?

00:09:45.718 --> 00:09:48.287
Are we collectively smoking something?

00:09:48.287 --> 00:09:50.523
Do we not know what we're doing?

00:09:50.523 --> 00:09:53.459
I suppose you might argue
that that's the case.

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:54.660
But the way to find out

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:58.064
is to find out whether or
not the gizmos that we make

00:09:58.064 --> 00:10:00.366
are really doing what
we say they are doing.

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:02.201
A few other teams

00:10:02.201 --> 00:10:05.237
have already tried to replicate
the MEGA drive's results,

00:10:05.237 --> 00:10:07.506
but the findings were negative.

00:10:07.506 --> 00:10:09.141
People don't expect this thing to work.

00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:10.710
It sounds so bizarre.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.578
There are several things that could happen

00:10:12.578 --> 00:10:14.513
that could cause these false positives.

00:10:14.513 --> 00:10:16.782
And that's what people
think is gonna happen.

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:19.385
If they're saying this strange
effect is actually real,

00:10:19.385 --> 00:10:20.753
then...

00:10:20.753 --> 00:10:23.990
and then somebody else comes
along and proves it wrong,

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:26.525
then they look like they have
egg on their face, basically.

00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:30.196
So it's much harder to say
that something is a real effect

00:10:30.196 --> 00:10:32.898
when it's controversial.

00:10:34.533 --> 00:10:36.135
Because of this controversy,

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:39.305
NASA is now funding an independent
study of the MEGA drive

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:42.008
at the Naval Research Laboratory.

00:10:42.008 --> 00:10:45.378
The neat thing about the
claim here is, they say

00:10:45.378 --> 00:10:47.680
there's some revolutionary new physics

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.349
that lets you vary your inertial mass,

00:10:50.349 --> 00:10:52.518
which means you can actually be heavier

00:10:52.518 --> 00:10:54.053
and lighter at different times.

00:10:54.053 --> 00:10:55.121
And if you can wiggle

00:10:55.121 --> 00:10:57.490
at the same time as
you're changing your mass,

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.625
now you've maybe got a way
to move yourself around

00:10:59.625 --> 00:11:02.094
and do some sort of wiggly,
wiggly, wiggly thing

00:11:02.094 --> 00:11:03.863
all through space.

00:11:03.863 --> 00:11:06.165
Now, is that possible in this universe?

00:11:07.066 --> 00:11:08.434
Maybe?

00:11:08.434 --> 00:11:09.635
Maybe not.

00:11:09.635 --> 00:11:11.604
Mike McDonald is part of the team

00:11:11.604 --> 00:11:13.005
testing the device,

00:11:13.005 --> 00:11:15.574
but even he admits that
conclusively proving

00:11:15.574 --> 00:11:18.811
or disproving this theory won't be easy.

00:11:18.811 --> 00:11:20.680
Is the test going to be conclusive?

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:23.683
Let me answer that
question with a question.

00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:25.217
What if I ask you to prove to me

00:11:25.217 --> 00:11:27.687
that there are no pink elephants?

00:11:27.687 --> 00:11:31.223
Now, you might say every
elephant I've ever seen is gray,

00:11:31.223 --> 00:11:34.393
but how do I know you just
haven't looked hard enough?

00:11:34.393 --> 00:11:37.363
The answer is that you can't
really prove a negative.

00:11:37.363 --> 00:11:39.432
Tests give us enough information

00:11:39.432 --> 00:11:42.368
to decide if the way we
think the world works

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:45.137
is valid enough for us to
keep going in that direction.

00:11:45.137 --> 00:11:48.207
And that's what this
sort of test can tell us.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:50.710
If you asked me to put on my betting hat

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:53.412
and say, are we really going to see

00:11:53.412 --> 00:11:55.548
exactly the effect that is claimed,

00:11:55.548 --> 00:11:58.317
and is it going to be so strong

00:11:58.317 --> 00:11:59.452
that we think, ah, yes,

00:11:59.452 --> 00:12:01.921
it's definitely the
mechanism that's claimed too?

00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:05.591
You know, I'd say that's a number

00:12:05.591 --> 00:12:08.127
probably somewhere between one and 10

00:12:08.127 --> 00:12:10.129
and one in 10 million,

00:12:10.129 --> 00:12:12.865
and I would lean toward the higher end.

00:12:12.865 --> 00:12:16.168
The odds, the odds are on no to be honest,

00:12:16.168 --> 00:12:20.639
but the odds are on no for
every new idea that comes up.

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:23.142
And remember that science isn't a tool

00:12:23.142 --> 00:12:24.810
that gets dirtied by use.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.546
You know, the scientific method
comes out of any encounter

00:12:27.546 --> 00:12:29.648
looking as shiny as where it started.

00:12:29.648 --> 00:12:31.751
And if we use it well,

00:12:31.751 --> 00:12:34.520
even if we don't find that
there's some new effect here,

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:35.855
we'll learn something

00:12:35.855 --> 00:12:38.357
about how to do these kinds
of tests better in the future

00:12:38.357 --> 00:12:40.126
for whatever is coming along next.

00:12:42.328 --> 00:12:44.296
There's a small but real chance

00:12:44.296 --> 00:12:45.664
that there's nothing to it.

00:12:46.632 --> 00:12:49.401
For me, it's exceedingly unlikely,

00:12:49.401 --> 00:12:51.403
but there's been more than one occasion

00:12:51.403 --> 00:12:54.740
where things haven't gone
right on a particular day

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:59.378
and I've started drafting
an apology to the people

00:12:59.378 --> 00:13:01.881
who are interested in this, saying

00:13:01.881 --> 00:13:03.983
"Gee guys, I'm really sorry.

00:13:03.983 --> 00:13:06.385
You know, it wasn't real after all."

00:13:06.385 --> 00:13:11.157
I haven't had one of those
episodes in a number of months.

00:13:13.959 --> 00:13:15.060
Assuming the device

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.163
passes its replication trials

00:13:17.163 --> 00:13:18.464
and nothing else can explain

00:13:18.464 --> 00:13:20.533
the forces the team is claiming,

00:13:20.533 --> 00:13:22.134
one day, the ultimate test

00:13:22.134 --> 00:13:23.969
would be for an array of MEGA drives

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:26.572
to maneuver a CubeSat in space.

00:13:26.572 --> 00:13:28.674
I'm 79 years old,

00:13:28.674 --> 00:13:31.844
so I don't know how
long I'm going to live.

00:13:31.844 --> 00:13:32.812
A few years,

00:13:32.812 --> 00:13:35.681
maybe I'll see something
in space, maybe I won't.

00:13:35.681 --> 00:13:37.316
If I live longer than that,

00:13:37.316 --> 00:13:39.985
I'm pretty sure I will
see something in space.

00:13:41.387 --> 00:13:43.856
Science fiction will be vindicated

00:13:43.856 --> 00:13:48.194
as transformed into scientific
fact in that regard.

